    "body": "Drilling on the Marimaca project site in Chile. Credit: Marimaca Copper.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 26, 2026\nChile has cleared Marimaca Copper (TSX: MARI; ASX: MC2) to begin early construction work at its namesake copper project near Antofagasta, two days after a unit of Sprott (TSX, NYSE: SII) backed a proposed financing package for construction.\nChile’s National Geology and Mining Service granted the mining-method permit to Marimaca’s subsidiary, Compañía Minera Cielo Azul Limitada. It allows site preparation and bulk earthmoving at the Marimaca Oxide Deposit (MOD) site. Full-scale construction still requires board approval, according to Thursday’s company release .\nReceipt of the permit “represents another derisking milestone for the MOD project,” BMO Capital Markets mining analyst Rene Cartier said Thursday in a note. “Marimaca is likely to receive additional sectorial permits in due course, though these are not viewed as critical path items.”\nThe permit builds on environmental approval granted in November 2025, “allowing for the commencement of the fully funded early works construction activities at the project,” said José Antonio Merino, Marimaca’s managing director in Chile.\n13-year mine\nThe open pit, about 45 km from Antofagasta, would produce an average of 48,000 tonnes of refined copper cathode a year over its first decade. Marimaca put the upfront cost at $587 million (C$834.7 million) and the mine life at 13 years in its 2025 feasibility study.\nSprott Resource Lending Corp.’s investment committee backed a proposed funding package of up to $450 million Tuesday. It includes up to $425 million in senior secured debt and up to $25 million in equity under a non-binding term sheet. The first $100 million of the loan would become available after a construction decision, subject to final agreements, due diligence and other conditions, Marimaca said.\nThe oxide deposit has measured and indicated resources of 213.5 million tonnes grading 0.40% copper, containing about 853,800 tonnes of copper, according to an August 2025 estimate. It also holds inferred resources of 21.2 million tonnes at 0.29% copper for about 62,200 tonnes of the metal. The measured and indicated resources include proven and probable reserves of 178.6 million tonnes grading 0.42% copper.\nMarimaca shares rose 2.4% to C$6.30 Thursday morning in Toronto, valuing the company at about C$854 million ($601 million). The shares have traded between C$5.53 and C$13.49 in the past 52 weeks.\nPrint\nTAG: Chile environment copper copper exploration environment\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Canada South America\nCOMPANY: Marimaca Copper\nRepublish this article",
